REGIONAL REGULATION OF KATINGAN REGENCY

NUMBER 1 OF 2023

CONCERNING

PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE

BY THE GRACE OF ALMIGHTY GOD

REGENT OF KATINGAN,

Considering:

a. that with the enactment of Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection as amended by Law Number 35 of 2014 concerning Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection and Law Number 24 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence, it is deemed necessary to prevent and overcome violence against Women and Children through legal protection for Women and Children victims of violence;
b. that based on the consideration as referred to in letter a, it is necessary to establish a Regional Regulation concerning the Protection of Women and Children victims of violence;

In View of:

1. Article 18 paragraph (6) of the 1945 Constitution of the Republic of Indonesia;
2. Law Number 5 of 2002 concerning the Establishment of Katingan Regency, Seruyan Regency, Sukamara Regency, Lamandau Regency, Gunung Mas Regency, Pulang Pisau Regency, Murung Raya Regency and Barito Timur Regency in the Province of Central Kalimantan (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 18, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4180);
3. Law Number 39 of 1999 concerning Human Rights (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 1999 Number 165, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
4. Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2002 Number 100, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4235) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 17 of 2016 concerning the Stipulation of Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 1 of 2016 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 23 of 2002 concerning Child Protection into Law (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2016 Number 237, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5946);
5. Law Number 23 of 2004 concerning the Elimination of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2004 Number 95, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4419);
6. Law Number 21 of 2007 concerning the Eradication of the Criminal Act of Trafficking in Persons (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2007 Number 58, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4720);
7. Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 82, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5234) as amended several times most recently by Law Number 13 of 2022 concerning the Second Amendment to Law Number 12 of 2011 concerning the Formulation of Legislation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 143, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6801);
8. Law Number 16 of 2011 concerning Legal Aid (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 104, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5246);
9. Law Number 23 of 2014 concerning Regional Government (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2014 Number 244, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 5587) as amended several times most recently by Government Regulation in Lieu of Law Number 2 of 2022 concerning Job Creation (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 238, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 6841);
10. Law Number 12 of 2022 concerning the Criminal Act of Sexual Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 120, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 3886);
11. Government Regulation Number 4 of 2006 concerning the Implementation and Cooperation of Recovery of Victims of Domestic Violence (State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia of 2006 Number 15, Supplement to the State Gazette of the Republic of Indonesia Number 4604);
12. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2008 concerning the Implementation of Women's Protection;
13. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 3 of 2008 concerning Guidelines for the Implementation of Child Protection;
14. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 5 of 2010 concerning Guidelines for the Establishment and Development of Integrated Service Centers (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2010 Number 466);
15. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for Handling Child Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 42);
16. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 19 of 2011 concerning Guidelines for the Empowerment of Women Victims of Violence (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2011 Number 903);
17. Regulation of the State Minister for Women's Empowerment and Child Protection Number 2 of 2022 concerning Service Standards for the Protection of Women and Children (State News of the Republic of Indonesia of 2022 Number 85);

With Mutual Approval

REGIONAL HOUSE OF REPRESENTATIVES OF KATINGAN REGENCY

and

REGENT OF KATINGAN

HAS DECIDED:

To Establish:

REGIONAL REGULATION CONCERNING THE PROTECTION OF WOMEN AND CHILDREN VICTIMS OF VIOLENCE.

CHAPTER II

PRINCIPLES, OBJECTIVES AND SCOPE OF REGULATION

Article 2

Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence is implemented based on the principles of:
a. respect and fulfillment of victims' rights;
b. justice and gender equality;
c. non-discrimination;
d. best interests of victims;
e. local wisdom;
f. right to life, survival and development of children;
g. respect for victims' opinions; and
h. legal certainty.

Article 4

The Scope of Regulation on Protection of Women and Children Victims of Violence includes:
a. victims' rights;
b. authority, obligations and responsibilities;
c. institutions;
d. minimum service standards;
e. social protection house;
f. reporting;
g. guidance and supervision;
h. community participation; and
i. funding.

CHAPTER III

VICTIMS' RIGHTS

Article 5

Every Woman and Child victim of violence has the right:
a. to have their dignity respected as a human being;
b. to receive protection from family, community, Regional Government and other parties either temporarily or based on the determination of a Protection order from the Court;
c. to receive recovery health services physically, psychologically and sexually according to the suffering experienced by victims of violence;
d. to receive special handling related to victim confidentiality;
e. to receive assistance by companions and legal aid at every level of the examination process in accordance with the provisions of laws and regulations;
f. to receive spiritual guidance services.

CHAPTER IV

AUTHORITY, OBLIGATIONS AND RESPONSIBILITIES

Article 7

1. The Regional Government has the authority and responsibility for the Protection of Women and Children victims of violence.
2. The obligations and responsibilities of the Regional Government as referred to in paragraph (1) include:
a. guaranteeing the rights of women and children without discrimination in accordance with laws and regulations;
b. implementing policies for the protection of Women and Children victims of violence based on laws and regulations;
c. establishing policies, programs and activities for the protection of Women and Children victims of violence;
d. providing support for facilities and infrastructure for the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;
e. allocating budget in the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;
f. fostering and supervising the implementation of protection of Women and Children victims of violence;
g. improving the quality and competence of human resources who provide services/assistance for victims of violence against women and children;
h. cooperating with the community and/or community organizations in the implementation of protection of women in accordance with laws and regulations;
i. taking preventive action in order to prevent violations of the rights of women and children.
3. The responsibility of the Regional Government in efforts to protect women and children as referred to in Article 7 paragraph (1) shall be carried out by Regional Apparatus in accordance with their duties and functions.

CHAPTER V

INSTITUTIONAL ARRANGEMENTS

Article 9

1. The established UPTD PPA may accommodate institutions/work units that provide services for victims of violence such as safe houses, social protection houses for Children, trauma center protection houses, women's social protection houses, shelters and other service activities in accordance with applicable Laws and Regulations.
2. Forms of services implemented by UPTD PPA include:
a. handling complaints;
b. health rehabilitation;
c. social rehabilitation;
d. legal aid;
e. recovery/psychological services;
f. mediation; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration.
3. The provision of integrated services as referred to in paragraph (2) shall be implemented in accordance with SPM.

CHAPTER VI

MINIMUM SERVICE STANDARDS

Article 10

1. SPM is implemented by the Regional Technical Implementation Unit for the Protection of Women and Children with the aim of ensuring that Women and Children victims of violence receive the minimum services needed.
2. SPM as referred to in paragraph (1) includes services for:
a. handling complaints/reports of victims of violence against women and children;
b. health rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
c. social rehabilitation for women and children victims of violence;
d. legal aid for women and children victims of violence;
e. recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;
f. mediation for women and children victims of violence; and
g. repatriation and social reintegration for women and children victims of violence.

Article 11

1. SPM as referred to in Article 9 paragraph (3) has performance indicators including:
a. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive complaint handling by trained officers within UPTD PPA;
b. coverage of Women and Children victims of violence who receive health services by trained health personnel at Community Health Centers and Hospitals;
c. coverage of social rehabilitation services provided by trained social rehabilitation officers for Women and Children victims of violence within UPTD PPA;
d. coverage of spiritual guidance services provided by trained spiritual guidance officers for Women and Children victims of violence within UPTD PPA;
e. coverage of legal aid services for women and children victims of violence;
f. coverage of recovery/psychological services for women and children victims of violence;
g. coverage of mediation services for women and children victims of violence;
h. coverage of repatriation services for women and children victims of violence; and
i. coverage of social reintegration services for women and children victims of violence.
2. The establishment of SPM UPTD PPA performance indicators for Women and Children victims of violence as referred to in paragraph (1) constitutes a minimum target that must be achieved.

CHAPTER IX

GUIDANCE AND SUPERVISION

Article 14

1. The Regent conducts guidance and supervision of the implementation of UPTD PPA.
2. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (1) includes:
a. guidelines and fulfillment standards;
b. technical guidance and training;
c. supervision;
d. provision of facilities;
e. monitoring; and
f. evaluation.
3. Guidance as referred to in paragraph (2) is in order to realize the objectives of protection of women and Children victims of violence in accordance with minimum service standards.

CHAPTER X

COMMUNITY PARTICIPATION

Article 15

1. In implementing protection for Women and Children victims of violence, the community may:
a. form family partners at the village and sub-district level in coordination with Damang or Mantir Adat and Community Leaders;
b. form women and child protection units within community organizations;
c. conduct socialization of the rights of Women and Children independently;
d. provide first aid to victims; and
e. report to the competent authorities if violence against victims occurs in their environment.
2. Community participation as referred to in paragraph (1) shall be carried out by individuals, family partners, community social institutions, non-governmental organizations, educational institutions, religious institutions, the private sector, and mass media.
